17592186045465 13194139535446 true] [17592186349604 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=24{x}^{2}+4x+2.\n$$" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349604 1113 #uuid "ffa40815-fb21-52fd-a6a4-4ce989aef44c" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349604 1145 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +7(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(14\\alpha +12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 14\\alpha +12\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349606 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349607 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349608 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349609 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349610 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1041 "selection" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349606 13194139535446 true] [17592186349605 1110 #uuid "59019122-b9a7-598f-a15f-7e1535e654c4" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349606 1034 "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535446 true] [17592186349606 1111 #uuid "9ce0bb76-fd3b-5d3c-be7e-037c818dd4f0" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349607 1034 "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}-{x}^{2}+1" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535446 true] [17592186349607 1111 #uuid "41bb8d40-e774-5ff5-b966-37eacfaaf29d" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349608 1034 "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}+2x+4" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349608 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535446 true] [17592186349608 1111 #uuid "1bfd82bd-902e-5a2d-a500-4d5ac0709fdf" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349609 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+{x}^{2}-2x+4" 13194139535446 true] [17592186349609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535446 true] [175921863496